 17592186045417] [17592186048584 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "abbdc596-7744-5a68-9999-a74874659d19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186050308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1031 "The Vector Equation of a Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1033 1376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1034 0.9613969874278565 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049485 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "f8b72b93-d1e8-5602-88e3-d4c903a3465a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050593 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050593 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050593 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050593 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186129160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050593 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186428547 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186050593 #edb/ref 1031 "Block Diagonalization of NxN Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050593 #edb/ref 1033 2019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050593 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "56f267b9-1121-55c5-a6c5-4c7ce248197a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128948 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128948 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/70/7014ce766be779f4231c78a99a6e013bfc48bfd9e21da7fa565c49e466437c99.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2 \\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PBP^{-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128948 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1cc03cef-e717-5ac1-ab9d-75508f3aa056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128948 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/5e/5e03c481312af462a0739b4670e24325d2e06cc10449c65bfb04208206fc5d58.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-1(-1)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n$\\lambda_1={\\color{red}-1}.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 5}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n![](images/71/712c3f663a367090ab5ba81705e7c7f36ce6d4e2d6e2b13cc59383421e408ea7.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128949 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5534" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128949 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n![](images/31/31a6051588cdb215a025179138803257d01f72cb2f231cda05cbbd67b56b0fb9.png)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=1-\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find the matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128949 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fb7c97e6-f106-5a47-ad01-75205c0d2603" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128949 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/2a/2a08ff9b84de36c2b9141809f385f688f6b8c83b2b916bd3469432705636a154.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](images/50/50b840579f0396b87913b773975c26b337c9846c139bc4a1bf9445984681f062.png)\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} .$ Therefore, \n\n![](images/55/55b4ecb56daf7f8cce19878af9f17e237a5f38b1593a4000d39487cb93ff2c95.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128950 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128950 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2 \\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n![](images/5f/5fd08491c00587e0efa4017850407c58af8ad0821c2c1aab512ec20b5295d128.png)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=7 + 4\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_3=-3 + 2\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find a possible matrix $P.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2b2a7fbc-8911-507f-915c-cf9b50653e5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128950 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}=\\overline{\\color{red}\\lambda}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\lambda_3}=\\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} = \\overline{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}4},$ \n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/c8/c8b3063b226f25c53f064e3f427446da7cc4c7cd99a20b8c1d9e46d33bd429e0.png)\nand\n![](images/04/0489fb7ddf3f73682e4e61345fa73a601c5f8ae01bf319918becd9c2426cc73d.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](images/35/350a5319ff61e3f794e780561616c7a9523f18832ee040ce8faa0825818f8f24.png)\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}.$ Therefore,\n![](images/c6/c6213ccfec2acbc449e38e431ef1d60d2e06b19066b735faa6426dfd4b326c9c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128978 #edb/ref 1069 "e-5906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128978 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/13/139905ee461ed3a946aa232e42e65f619f1550884b24dc72354303e3af7a05df.png)\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da515ea7-f4b0-58c5-adab-c826563dd3cf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186128978 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/66/66747ae8628c72623aa51610ed39af91154d06d61e67189f93d04079b64f494a.png)\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n![](images/ee/eef90c4d866110a74a221ce82ad275de5b07ddbf6e33213d94cf5f7831295022.png)\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_{1,2}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\color{red}\\lambda_3=-3}.$\n\n- From our matrix \n![](images/54/5449e99fabebae1514f40bdf40698e84bb60a578e495e5a363ed7ac617749ad2.png)\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n![](images/cd/cdf7fb1cb310f63e0dcf468aa045dc0fb1bb3b2e757ae431ae499c898ab5538f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129137 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048195 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129137 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129683 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129137 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129137 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129137 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129137 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129137 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129137 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1