9 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129139 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e5ccd12c-a814-5ebb-985c-eef8707001f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186129688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186269069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186400723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Eigenvalues and Corresponding Eigenvectors of a Matrix in Block Diagonal Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186128978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129160 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6704692a-2219-559f-9352-97c5fb590894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186129987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186130015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1045 "Block Diagonalization of NxN Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186129982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129362 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "427e5f35-5438-583c-9454-df8e3ae0c517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129685 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129685 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38660" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129685 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129685 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}5+7\\text{i} \\\\ 1+\\text{i} \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2,$  respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186129685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97813176-8f27-5690-98e4-6d6c62e9f0d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129685 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186129688 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129688 #edb/ref 1069 "q-38665" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129688 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129688 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186129688 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "582f937c-6cfb-53b4-966a-6cea9b2086e9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129688 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\,P=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{2,3}=-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{2,3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186129982 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129982 #edb/ref 1061 20173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129982 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2079dce9-f014-5601-a83c-150fae42ef41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129982 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129983 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129983 #edb/ref 1061 20174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129983 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6045c7fa-0f99-5ffd-9594-e626c92e4020" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129983 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129984 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186130015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129984 #edb/ref 1061 20175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129984 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9ac92ee1-74aa-5b6d-a07e-d4fc3f68f30b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129984 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129985 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129985 #edb/ref 1061 20176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129985 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5d6954b7-9d4b-57e8-aee6-999d162199be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129985 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129986 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129986 #edb/ref 1061 20177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129986 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "695a49a5-60af-5017-b281-1870ceb33c6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129986 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186130358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129987 #edb/ref 1061 20178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129987 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d7512dc9-649d-5cd3-992b-9f3d84b178e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186129987 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130015 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186129983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130015 #edb/ref 1061 21723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130015 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9a08b734-6db2-5bf9-8aae-178359e681f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130015 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186129160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130356 #edb/ref 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **block diagonalizable** if it is similar to a block diagonal matrix $B.$ That is, there exists a block diagonal matrix $B$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PBP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n \n\nis block diagonalizable because it can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, the matrix $A$ has eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\lambda_1 = 2},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2=3-2\\textrm i},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_3=3+2\\textrm i}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda_3}.$\nThe eigenvectors of ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_3},$ respectively, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAgain, notice that $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}_3}.$\nThe first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1},$ and the second and third columns correspond to the real and the imaginary parts of ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186130356 #edb/ref 1065 3597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130356 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8edf8405-33c8-5922-8946-6f87b2881781" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130356 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130357 #edb/ref 1064 "We have the following theorem regarding the block diagonalization of a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix:\n\n> *If $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A=PBP^{-1},\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *where the matrices $B$ and $P$ are given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}\n> $$\n\nIn other words, if $A$ is a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue and two complex eigenvalues, then $A$ is block diagonalizable.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The block of $B$ constructed from the real and imaginary parts of $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We can write the blocks of $B$ in either order. However, the order of the columns of $P$ *must* correspond to the order of the blocks." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130357 #edb/ref 1065 3598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130357 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "56c5960d-6197-586e-850e-8f1a23e2289a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130357 #edb/ref 1067 "The Block Diagonalization Theorem for a 3x3 Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130358 #edb/ref 1064 "We can find a block diagonalization for any real $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A.$ This means that for any square matrix $A,$ we can construct a block diagonal matrix $B$ and invertible matrix $P$ such that \n\n$$\nA=PBP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere the blocks of $B$ are either:\n\n- $1\\!\\times\\! 1$ blocks containing the real eigenvalues of $A,$ or\n\n- $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ blocks containing the rotation-scaling matrices corresponding to the complex (not real) eigenvalues of $A.$\n\nThe columns of $P$ form bases of the eigenspaces for the real eigenvectors, or come in the pairs $\\left(\\textrm{Re}(\\mathbf{v}), \\textrm{Im}(\\mathbf{v})\\right)$ for the non-real eigenvectors.\n\nLet's see an example of block diagonalization for higher-dimensional matrices." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130358 #edb/ref 1065 3599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130358 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "650f30ea-d42a-5fba-bd00-3f269007df84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186130358 #edb/ref 1067 "Generalization of Block Diagonalization to NxN Matrices" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266288 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\lambda =8\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}5\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ ∓7\\text{i} \\\\ 5∓3\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186266288 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266288 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11504871-6e11-558c-a4a9-c918ea05ff9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266337 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186266337 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266337 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "175ad214-847b-57ec-ba0a-35665534ee96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266339 #edb/ref 1080 "$\\lambda =3\\pm 8\\text{i}$ and $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1∓4\\text{i} \\\\ ∓7\\text{i} \\\\ 3\\pm 5\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186266339 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266339 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1797afb6-8aac-566a-b295-c8e35e784155" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266416 #edb/ref 1080 "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -3 \\\\ 3 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186266416 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417]